==Latin==

===Etymology===
Uncertain, but some have connected it to {{etyl|ine-pro|la}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₃osk-||ash tree|}}, the same source as Welsh ''onnen'', Latin {{term|ornus||wild mountain ash|lang=la}}, Lithuanian {{term|úosis|lang=lt}}, Russian {{term|ясень||jásen’|lang=ru}}, Albanian {{term|ah||beech|lang=sq}}, Ancient Greek {{term|ὀξύα||beech|lang=grc}}, Old Armenian {{term|հացի|tr=hacʿi|lang=xcl}}.  The connection stems from the assumption that Indo-Europeans used hollowed out ash trees as boats and skiffs.<ref>Schrader, Prehistoric antiquities of the Aryan peoples: a manual of comparative philology and the earliest culture</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-pronunc|caudex}}

===Alternative forms===
* [[codex#Latin|cōdex]]

===Noun===
{{la-noun|caudex|caudicis|caudicis|m|third}}

# A [[tree trunk]], [[stump]].
# A [[bollard]]; [[post]].
# A [[book]], [[writing]]; [[notebook]], [[account book]].
# {{context|pejorative|lang=la}} A bollard, [[blockhead]], [[idiot]].

